# Quality inspect and run

Owner playbook: [`docs/quality-checkpoints.md`](../../docs/quality-checkpoints.md). The tooling lives only in `vantage-main-server/ops/quality/`. Other repositories opt in with a `.quality.config.json`; no logic is copied there.

Hooks only record session state (busy / idle / ended) and start the worker. They do **not** review code. After a turn ends, the worker waits for 120 s of quiet (20 s after a session end), then runs the gate, triage and actions.

## Inspect

```bash
pnpm quality:status
```

Then read, under `git rev-parse --git-path agent-quality`:

- `latest.md`: the last finished run in one screen
- `triage.jsonl`: every gate and triage decision (`unchanged`, `noise`, `trivial`, `docs-only`, `skip`, `act`, `triage-failed`)
- `runs/<id>/report.json`: status, stage, decision, touched files, checks
- `runs/<id>/triage-brief.md`, `triage.out*`, `clean.md`, `docs.md`: model input and output
- `runs/<id>/checks/*.log`: typecheck, lint and tests (`*.input.log` is the re-run on unedited input)
- `runs/<id>/changes.patch`: what was or would be applied

Machine-wide files are in `~/.agent-quality/`: `worker.log`, `hook-errors.log`, `sessions/` and `notices/`.

Run statuses: `applied`, `skipped`, `dry-run`, `no-changes`, `stale-pending`, `stale`, `patch-ready`, `failed`.

```text
❌ Re-run the hook expecting a review
✅ pnpm quality:status, then open latest.md / runs/<id>/
```

## Run

```bash
pnpm finish-work                          # provider detected; --provider claude|cursor|codex to choose
pnpm finish-work --actions clean,docs     # skip triage
pnpm finish-work --dry-run                # triage only
pnpm quality:apply <run-id>               # apply a stale patch
pnpm quality:pause / quality:resume
pnpm quality:prune --dry-run
pnpm quality:install                      # after moving the checkout or changing hook definitions
```

Skip all of this when `AGENT_QUALITY_CHILD=1`.

`pnpm quality:test` runs offline. `pnpm quality:smoke <provider>` is a real-model run on a throwaway repository; never point it at application code.
